study_1/Fang2024.pdf.md:42:Psychiatric disorders exhibit a shared neuropathology, yet the diverse presentations among patients necessitate the identification of transdiagnostic subtypes to enhance diagnostic and treatment strategies. This study aims to unveil  potential  transdiagnostic  subtypes  based  on  personalized  gray  matter  morphological  abnormalities.  A total of 496 patients with psychiatric disorders and 255 healthy controls (HCs) from three distinct datasets (one for discovery and two for validation) were enrolled. Individualized gray matter morphological abnormalities were determined using normative modeling to identify transdiagnostic subtypes. In the discovery dataset, two transdiagnostic subtypes with contrasting patterns of structural abnormalities compared to HCs were identified. Reproducibility and generalizability analyses  demonstrated that these  subtypes could be generalized to  new patients and even to new disorders in the validation datasets. These subtypes were characterized by distinct disease epicenters. The gray matter abnormal pattern in subtype 1 was mainly linked to excitatory receptors, whereas subtype 2 showed a predominant association with inhibitory receptors. Furthermore, we observed that the gray matter abnormal pattern in subtype 2 was correlated with transcriptional profiles of inflammationrelated genes, while subtype 1 did not show this association. Our findings reveal two robust transdiagnostic biotypes, offering novel insights into psychiatric nosology.
study_1/Fang2024.pdf.md:62:regions (Chang et al., 2018). However, psychiatric disorders are highly heterogeneous syndromes and this variability among cases results in inconsistent or conflicting findings (Chen et al., 2016). Even within a single diagnosis, neuroimaging studies have reported reduced, increased, or even no differential gray matter volumes (L ´ azaro et al., 2011; Okasha et al., 2000). These results suggest the existence of distinct neuroanatomical subtypes in psychiatric disorders. Indeed, studies have identified subtypes with opposing patterns of structural abnormalities within a single diagnosis, such  as schizophrenia and  obsessivecompulsive  disorder  (Chand  et  al.,  2020;  Han  et  al.,  2022b).  To address this heterogeneity, normative modeling has been proposed to characterize structural abnormalities at the individual level by assessing deviations from the normal distribution (Marquand et al., 2016). Using these  individualized  structural  deviations  as  features,  a  recent  study successfully identified two depression biotypes with distinct patterns of cortical thinning and treatment responses (Li et al., 2024). However, whether individualized structural deviations could reveal transdiagnostic subtypes remains unknown.
study_1/Fang2024.pdf.md:66:In this study, we aimed to identify distinct transdiagnostic subtypes based  on  individualized  gray  matter  morphological  abnormalities compared to normal expectations. We included a total of 496 patients with psychiatric disorders and 255 healthy controls from three independent datasets (one discovery dataset and two validation datasets). First,  we  derived  individualized  deviation  maps  (individualized  gray matter  morphological  abnormalities)  using  normative  modeling  and used  them  to  determine  transdiagnostic  subtypes  in  the  discovery dataset. We then conducted a series of sensitivity analyses to examine the  reproducibility  and  generalizability  of  the  identified  subtypes  in unseen patients and even in unseen disorders in the validation datasets. Subsequently, we investigated the disease epicenters of the identified subtypes. To gain deeper insights into the molecular and genetic underpinnings of the subtypes, we further investigated the associations between structural abnormalities in these subtypes and the distribution of neurotransmitter receptors/transporters, as well as the transcriptional profiles of inflammation-related genes. The workflow of this study is shown in Fig. 1.
study_1/Fang2024.pdf.md:74:Fig. 1. The workflow of this study. For each brain region, individualized gray matter volume/morphological (GMV) abnormalities are obtained using normative modeling and used to identify transdiagnostic subtypes. A series of sensitivity analyses are conducted to assess the reproducibility and generalizability of clustering outcomes. To gain deeper insights into the subtypes, we investigate their disease epicenters, and molecular and genetic underpinnings.
study_1/Fang2024.pdf.md:84:First,  we  estimated  individualized  gray  matter  morphological  abnormalities using the normative modeling. Following methods used in previous  studies  (Han  et  al.,  2023a;  Marquand  et  al.,  2016;  Wolfers et al., 2018), we trained a Gaussian process regression model to estimate the normative range of regional gray matter volumes (GMVs) based on age and sex in HCs. This trained model was then applied to each patient, generating predicted GMVs. Subsequently, Z scores (representing individualized abnormalities) were calculated to quantify deviations of the predicted GMVs from normal distributions. Positive Z scores indicated higher GMVs in patients compared to HCs, and vice versa. This process resulted in a Z -score vector (246 × 1) for each patient, representing their individualized gray matter morphological abnormalities.
study_1/Fang2024.pdf.md:275:- Han, S., Cui, Q., Zheng, R., Li, S., Zhou, B., Fang, K., Sheng, W., Wen, B., Liu, L., Wei, Y., Chen, H., 2023a. Parsing altered gray matter morphology of depression using a framework integrating the normative model and non-negative matrix factorization. 14, 4053.
study_1/Fang2024.pdf.md:303:- Marquand, A.F., Rezek, I., Buitelaar, J., Beckmann, C.F., 2016. Understanding heterogeneity in clinical cohorts using normative models: beyond case-control studies. Biol. Psychiatry 80, 552 -561.
study_1/Fang2024.pdf.md:344:- Wolfers, T., Doan, N.T., Kaufmann, T., Alnæs, D., Moberget, T., Agartz, I., Buitelaar, J.K., Ueland, T., Melle, I., Franke, B., Andreassen, O.A., Beckmann, C.F., Westlye, L.T., Marquand, A.F., 2018. Mapping the heterogeneous phenotype of schizophrenia and bipolar disorder using normative models. JAMA Psychiatry 75, 1146 -1155.


